    {
        "title": "The Innovator's Dilemma",
        "author": "Clayton M. Christensen",
        "year": 1997,
        "genre": "science",
        "mood": ["hustle mode", "career pivot"],
        "summary": "Christensen, a Harvard Business School professor, set out to explain a paradox that classical management theory could not handle: why well-run, customer-focused, financially disciplined companies repeatedly lose their markets to upstarts producing technically inferior products. Drawing on detailed case studies of the disk-drive industry — where successive generations of smaller, slower drives toppled incumbents from mainframe-class manufacturers down to PC suppliers — and extending the argument to mechanical excavators, steel mini-mills, retail discount stores, and personal computers, he develops the concept of disruptive innovation. Sustaining innovations improve products along the dimensions established customers already value, and incumbents are typically excellent at delivering them. Disruptive innovations begin underserving the mainstream on traditional metrics while excelling on a new metric, often price, size, or simplicity, and they capture footholds in markets the leaders consider too small to bother with. Because resource-allocation processes inside large firms route investment toward the highest-margin, largest-volume opportunities visible to current customers, the very practices that make incumbents successful prevent them from responding when the disruptor's trajectory eventually crosses into the mainstream. Christensen frames this not as managerial incompetence but as a structural trap and offers prescriptions: spinning out autonomous units small enough to be excited by tiny markets, matching organizational scale to market scale, and discovering rather than forecasting demand for products that have no precedent. The book reshaped how Silicon Valley, venture capital, and corporate strategy departments think about technological change, and its central distinction between sustaining and disruptive innovation entered the language of business.",
    },
